for eight years Hugh Laurie starred in  the title role in the Fox medical drama  house but despite becoming an  international celebrity he isn't chasing  success for its own sake he is why he  hasn't been the same since house for an  actor not every day on the job is  glamorous and exciting sometimes they  can feel a little repetitive so after  nearly a decade playing dr. Gregory  House Hugh Laurie definitely wanted a  change of scenery he still enjoyed  playing the role all the way through to  the final season but the routine was  starting to get monotonous and he wasn't  sure if he was really cut out for that  kind of commitment anymore as the final  season was airing he told The Telegraph  you know we have done 170 episodes now I  think that's about 50 to 60 feature  films worth you want to break you really  do  so Laurie's first order of business  after house wrapped was to take a long  break from acting he ended up spending a  full three years away from Hollywood  save for a few small exceptions like  lending his voice of video game  LittleBigPlanet 3 he finally got back in  front of the camera in 2015 for the  Disney film Tomorrowland he loved  playing house but he often felt like he  was working himself to the bone on set  getting up at 5 a.m. for a 16-hour work  day was not uncommon when he stuck with  his schedule for nearly a decade no  wonder he needed an extended vacation  once the cameras stopped rolling how are  things in hell is the humidity the big  issue although Laurie would certainly  have preferred more privacy than the  press afforded him after the success of  house the overwhelming praise for the  series gave him a major confidence boost  after all he'd never been part of such  an internationally successful show  before previously he occasionally felt  insecure about his career because he'd  never gone to drama school and wondered  if he wasn't a genuinely qualified actor  Laurie used to question whether or not  he really belongs in the entertainment  industry  but once Hal sarkoff with audiences and  he saw the impact the show was having he  realized that it definitely earned his  place  it was a huge turning point in his  career as he told the Guardian House was  something I didn't have to apologize for  it was something I was really proud of  and whether you liked it or not it was  undeniable although Laurie wasn't acting  much right after house went off the air  he didn't completely stop working he  just chose to invest his time in a  different creative pursuit it turns out  he's a talented blues musician and he  loves recording music even more than he  loves acting in 2011 Laurie released let  them talk its first blues album he was  surprised by the success achieved and he  he went on to release a second album in  2013 entitled it didn't of rain he  admitted that he was quite nervous in  the recording studio but as it turned  out he had nothing to be worried about  his concerts sold out and the albums  were certified gold he told the Guardian  in 2013 there's a sensual pleasure  involved in making sounds harmonious  sounds that I just can't get and I don't  think anyone can quite get from acting  it just cannot get better than that  it takes over my whole body in a way  that acting just can't dr. Gregory House  was known for being prickly and brutally  honest but it would be tough to argue  that he was a villain despite his rough  exterior he had a kind heart and he  genuinely wanted to help his patients  but the actor who played him eventually  wanted to play a truly evil character in  2016 Hugh Laurie finally got the chance  when he landed the role of Richard  Roeper on the BBC series the night  manager based on john lecarre a's novel  of the same name the plot follows a  former soldier who manages to worm his  way into ropers in a circle as Roper is  aiming the Egyptian government in the  midst of the Arab Spring  at one point Roper is described as the  worst man in the world and Laurie was up  for the challenge of bringing this vile  character to life he told The Telegraph  villainy serves a purpose and all good  storytelling it's a very well trodden  path in all kinds of stories so when I  was offered the chance to play Roper  there was no way I could possibly pass  it up a life depends on the quality of  your performance in the next half-hour  so smile after eight years of playing  house  Laurie knew that he could easily land  another doctor role if he wanted to but  he felt like doing so would mean he was  basically putting himself in a box but  when he was offered the part of doctor  Eldon chance on the Hulu series chance  he just couldn't pass it up  when Astin a 2016 interview with junkie  if he hesitated before taking another  medical role he answered for about two  minutes  I opened the script and thoughts oh this  is good it's a shame I can't do it  because it's medical after three pages  are forgotten  Laurie made an exception this time a  constructed shunt was a different type  of doctor who was dealing with very  different patients than dr. house was  you know I'm not a treating therapist  I'm a consulting or a psychiatrist sense  of the medical drama and a suspenseful  thriller all wrapped up in one and  Laurie didn't feel like he was playing  the same role all over again overall  he's certainly changed in the years  since the house finale but the  authenticity that he brings there  Rolle still makes them one of the best  actors in the business check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos but your favorite  actors are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and to the Bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

